/*==============================================================================
🏷️  SystemVerilog Clocking Block
==============================================================================

🎯 Purpose:
------------
Clocking blocks provide *deterministic synchronization* between the Testbench (TB) and the Design Under Test (DUT).
They eliminate race conditions by defining when TB should drive and sample signals relative to the clock edge.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🧭 1️⃣ Why We Need Clocking Blocks
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
• In simulation, TB and DUT execute concurrently.
• If both try to read/write the same signal at the same simulation time, race conditions occur → unpredictable results.

✅ Clocking blocks solve this by introducing timing separation:
  - DUT acts exactly at the clock edge.
  - TB acts slightly later, according to defined skews (#delays).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🧩 2️⃣ Syntax Overview
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
clocking cb @(posedge clk);
  default input #1ns output #2ns; // input sample delay, output drive delay
  input  q;    // TB reads DUT output (1 ns after edge)
  output d;    // TB drives DUT input (2 ns after edge)
endclocking

• `@(posedge clk)` → defines timing domain (posedge of clk).
• `input #1ns`  → TB samples outputs 1 ns after clock edge.
• `output #2ns` → TB drives inputs 2 ns after clock edge.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🕓 3️⃣ Timing Reference (Non-Additive Skews)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All skews are measured *from the same clock edge (time = 0)*.
They are **not** cumulative.

  Time (ns): | 0      1      2      3 ... 10
  Clock:     | ↑            ↓            ↑
              posedge clk          next posedge
  --------------------------------------------------------------
  Event:
   0 ns  → DUT samples input d
   +1 ns → TB samples output q  (input skew)
   +2 ns → TB drives new d      (output skew)
   10 ns → DUT sees new d at next posedge
  --------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Common doubt:
 “If TB samples at +1 ns and drives at +2 ns, shouldn’t that be 3 ns?”
 → No. Both 1 ns and 2 ns are relative to the same reference point
   (the posedge clk). They are *independent* offsets.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🧩 4️⃣ Testbench Usage Example
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
initial begin
  cb.d <= 0;          // Drives after +2 ns (per output skew)
  @(cb);              // Wait for next clocking event (posedge clk)
  cb.d <= 1;          // Again drives after +2 ns
  @(cb);              // Waits another clock edge
  $display("At %0t: q=%0b", $time, cb.q); // Reads q at +1 ns (input skew)
  #5 $finish;
end

🧠 Line-by-line meaning:
 • `cb.d <= 0;` schedules drive 2 ns after clock edge.
 • `@(cb);` waits for next clock event safely (like @(posedge clk)).
 • `cb.q` reads q 1 ns after edge — already stable.
 • TB and DUT never access same signal simultaneously.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
⚙️ 5️⃣ Complete Flow per Clock Cycle
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Time | Actor | Action |
|------|--------|----------------------------------------------|
| 0 ns | DUT | Samples d (old value) |
| +1 ns | TB  | Reads q safely (input #1ns) |
| +2 ns | TB  | Drives new d (output #2ns) |
|10 ns | DUT | Samples new d on next posedge |

✅ Result: No race conditions — deterministic simulation.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
💎 6️⃣ Real DV / UVM Usage
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
• Every UVM virtual interface includes a clocking block.
• All driver and monitor transactions go through it for timing accuracy.
• Example in interface:
    interface axi_if(input logic clk);
      clocking cb @(posedge clk);
        default input #1ns output #2ns;
        input ready; output valid, data;
      endclocking
    endinterface

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🧠 7️⃣ Key Takeaways
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
• Clocking block = timing “contract” between TB and DUT.
• Avoids races automatically — no manual #delays needed.
• Skews are relative to clock edge (not additive).
• Provides clear, maintainable, protocol-accurate testbenches.
• Essential in UVM and professional verification environments.

==============================================================================*/
